@startuml
' Figure 5.8 – System sequence diagram: what publishing sets in motion
' The writer's request returns before any of the expensive work happens. This is
' the figure that answers "why is anything a background job at all".
!include ../_style.puml
mainframe interaction SequenceDiagram Publish pipeline
autonumber "0 :"

participant "Writer" as R
participant "System" as A
participant "Database" as D
participant "Queue" as Q
participant "Worker" as K
participant "Embedding\nprovider" as E
participant "Language\nmodel" as L

ref over R, L : Sign in

R -> A : POST /articles/:id/publish\nwith placement and visibility
activate A
A -> A : Check eligibility for premium\nor marketplace placement
A -> D : Write the article row, the generated tsvector,\nthe word count and the read time
activate D
D --> A : Stored
deactivate D
A -> Q : Enqueue embed-article
A --> R : 201, the article is live
deactivate A
note right of R
  The writer waits for one database write, not for
  an embedding round trip. Everything below happens
  after the response has been sent.
end note

|||

Q -> K : embed-article
activate K
K -> D : Delete the article's existing chunks
note right of K
  Delete before insert: the unique constraint on
  (article_id, chunk_index) makes an update
  impossible, so a re-publish replaces the set.
end note
K -> K : Split into chunks of 120–1200 characters,\nprefixing headings, splitting on sentence boundaries
loop for each chunk
  K -> E : Embed the chunk
  activate E
  E --> K : Vector, 1536 dimensions
  deactivate E
end
K -> D : Insert the chunks with their vectors
K -> D : Delete the writer's cached portfolio insight
K -> Q : Chain extract-writer-memory
deactivate K

Q -> K : extract-writer-memory
activate K
K -> D : Read the writer's ten most recent published articles
activate D
D --> K : Corpus sample
deactivate D
K -> L : Ask for tone, style, vocabulary and topics\nas a schema-validated object
activate L
L --> K : Structured profile
deactivate L
K -> D : Upsert the writer's voice profile
deactivate K

note over R, L
  The memory job is chained after embedding rather than
  enqueued beside it: it reads the chunks the first job
  writes, so in parallel it would race and lose on a
  writer's first publish.
end note
@enduml
